WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.407
Environments change.

2
00:00:01.407 --> 00:00:02.385
Threats evolve.

3
00:00:02.385 --> 00:00:04.172
Business processes adapt.

4
00:00:04.172 --> 00:00:06.257
Detection rules should evolve as well.

5
00:00:06.257 --> 00:00:14.469
A rule that worked well last quarter may need changes after a new system, new logging pipeline, or new operational pattern appears.

6
00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:25.793
Tuning may involve refining conditions, updating exclusions, adjusting thresholds, improving enrichment, changing severity, adding triage notes, or revising documentation.

7
00:00:25.793 --> 00:00:29.615
The goal is to improve value without hiding meaningful activity.

8
00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:33.357
Operational feedback is one of the best sources of improvement.

9
00:00:33.357 --> 00:00:42.636
Analysts can identify confusing alerts, repeated false positives, missing context, and signals that require too much manual work.

10
00:00:42.636 --> 00:00:46.577
Detection engineers can use that feedback to make rules more useful.

11
00:00:46.577 --> 00:00:50.618
Continuous improvement turns detections into living defensive assets.

12
00:00:50.618 --> 00:01:00.795
Review cycles, ownership, version history, testing notes, and performance metrics help teams keep rules aligned with changing threats and changing environments.
